F002 | Remaining Length | Remaining Length counts variable-header plus payload bytes but excludes its own encoding. It is base-128 variable length: each byte contributes its low seven bits and bit 7 signals continuation; encodings use at most four bytes, representing 0 through 268435455. A representation that continues beyond four bytes is malformed.
F003 | MQTT fixed-header flags | For reserved fixed-header flag bits, the receiver must require the table-specified value; receipt of invalid fixed-header flags requires closing the Network Connection.
F004 | CONNECT sequencing | The first Client-to-Server packet after establishing a Network Connection must be CONNECT. A Client may send CONNECT only once on that connection; a second CONNECT is a protocol violation and the Server disconnects the Client.
F005 | CONNECT variable header | CONNECT variable-header fields are Protocol Name, Protocol Level, Connect Flags, and Keep Alive in that order. MQTT 3.1.1 uses Protocol Name MQTT and Protocol Level 4; the reserved Connect Flags bit must be zero. Unsupported protocol level is answered with CONNACK return code 0x01 and then disconnect.
F006 | Clean Session | With CleanSession=1, Client and Server discard previous Session state and start a new Session lasting only for the Network Connection; state associated with that Session must not be reused in a subsequent Session. Server Session state includes the Client's subscriptions.
F007 | Client Identifier | CONNECT payload begins with a required ClientId encoded as an MQTT UTF-8 string. The Server must support IDs 1–23 encoded bytes comprising ASCII letters and digits; the standard additionally permits (but does not require) supporting IDs longer than 23 bytes and characters outside that set. A zero-byte ID is optional for a Server to support; if supported, it requires CleanSession=1 and the Server assigns a unique ID.
F008 | Packet Identifier | Packet Identifiers are two bytes with MSB first. SUBSCRIBE must carry a nonzero 16-bit Packet Identifier, and SUBACK must contain the identifier used by its corresponding SUBSCRIBE. QoS 0 PUBLISH must not contain a Packet Identifier.
F009 | CONNACK successful response | The first Server-to-Client packet must be CONNACK. CONNACK has Remaining Length 2, a one-byte acknowledgement-flags field whose reserved bits 7–1 are zero, and a one-byte return code. When accepting CleanSession=1, the Server must set Session Present to 0 and return code 0; CONNACK has no payload.
F010 | QoS 0 PUBLISH | PUBLISH variable header begins with a UTF-8 Topic Name, which must not contain wildcard characters. For QoS 0, no Packet Identifier is present, and a zero-length payload is valid. The QoS 0 DUP bit must be zero. A PUBLISH with both QoS bits set is invalid and requires closing the connection; a QoS 0 receiver sends no response.
F011 | SUBSCRIBE payload | SUBSCRIBE payload is a contiguous list of at least one UTF-8 Topic Filter followed by a Requested QoS byte for each filter. The upper six bits of each Requested QoS byte must be zero; QoS values must be 0, 1, or 2, otherwise the Server closes the Network Connection. The Server should support wildcard filters, and if it elects not to, it must reject filters containing wildcards. A Server must return one ordered SUBACK code per filter; code 0x00 grants maximum QoS 0.
F012 | QoS 0 subscription delivery | A Server sends PUBLISH to each Client with a matching subscription. If overlapping subscriptions match the same Client, it must deliver at the maximum QoS of the matching subscriptions and may send additional copies; the Server may begin matching delivery before sending SUBACK. Subscription replacement for an identical filter replaces the previous subscription without interrupting publications.
F013 | PINGREQ/PINGRESP | PINGREQ has fixed-header type 12 with reserved flags zero, Remaining Length 0, no variable header and no payload. The Server must send PINGRESP in response; PINGRESP has type 13, reserved flags zero, Remaining Length 0, and no variable header or payload.
F014 | DISCONNECT | DISCONNECT is a Client-to-Server final Control Packet with type 14, zero reserved flags, Remaining Length 0, no variable header or payload. After sending it the Client must close the connection and send no further packets; on receipt the Server should close the connection if the Client has not already done so.
F015 | MQTT UTF-8 encoded strings | UTF-8 strings are length-prefixed with a two-byte length and have byte lengths 0–65535. Encoded characters must be well-formed UTF-8 and must not include U+0000. U+0001–U+001F, U+007F–U+009F, and Unicode non-characters SHOULD NOT be included; these are discouraged, not a closed exhaustive character allowlist.
F016 | MQTT over TCP framing | MQTT requires an ordered, lossless byte stream in each direction. A Control Packet has a fixed header followed by any variable header and payload; Remaining Length gives the byte count of the latter two parts, excluding its own encoding, so packet boundaries are determined by MQTT packet lengths rather than TCP read boundaries.
F017 | QoS 0 delivery and message ordering | A QoS 0 sender MUST send PUBLISH with DUP=0; the receiver accepts ownership on receipt, sends no response, and the sender does not retry. By default each Topic is ordered; for an ordered Topic, the Server MUST deliver according to the ordering rules and send PUBLISH packets to consumers for the same Topic and QoS in the order received from any given Client.
F018 | Topic Filter matching | Topic levels are separated by '/'; adjacent separators represent an empty level. '#' matches zero or more levels and is valid only alone or after a separator as the final filter character; '+' matches exactly one level and must occupy a whole level. Topic Names and Topic Filters are nonempty and case-sensitive; matching does not normalize or modify them, and each non-wildcarded level matches character-for-character. A filter beginning with '#' or '+' MUST NOT match a Topic Name beginning with '$'.
F019 | Connection-local protocol errors | Unless otherwise specified, when a Client or Server detects a protocol violation, it MUST close the Network Connection on which the offending Control Packet was received. A transient error while processing an inbound Control Packet likewise requires closing that connection; when a Server detects a transient error, it SHOULD NOT disconnect or otherwise affect interactions with any other Client.
