F03 MQTT 16-bit integer values MQTT 16-bit integer values are transmitted big-endian, with the most significant byte first.
F04 MQTT UTF-8 encoded strings Text fields are UTF-8 strings prefixed by a two-byte byte-length, with lengths 0–65535 unless a field specifies otherwise. Encoded characters must be well-formed UTF-8 and must not encode U+0000; an ill-formed string or U+0000 in a received packet requires closing the connection. Certain control/non-character code points are discouraged and may be grounds for closing, not an exhaustive forbidden set.
F05 MQTT Network Connection MQTT requires an underlying transport that supplies an ordered, lossless byte stream in both directions. TCP/IP is suitable; connectionless UDP alone is unsuitable because it can lose or reorder data.
F06 MQTT Packet Identifier Packet Identifiers are two-byte values. SUBSCRIBE must carry a nonzero 16-bit identifier, and the corresponding SUBACK must echo it; a QoS 0 PUBLISH carries no Packet Identifier.
F07 Client CONNECT ordering and multiplicity After establishing a Network Connection, a Client's first packet must be CONNECT. It may send CONNECT only once on that connection; a second CONNECT is a protocol violation and the Server disconnects the Client.
