EVENT {"TRIGGER": "Synchronous broker_run call from main after valid port configuration.", "PRECONDITION": "config is non-NULL, port is nonzero and host-order; caller keeps config valid until return. Linux C99 process context.", "INPUT": "Borrowed broker_config. This function creates listener and owns all accepted client_state objects, poll descriptors, input buffers, subscriptions and output FIFOs for the call duration.", "ACTION": "Initialize stop flag, install SIGINT/SIGTERM handlers that ONLY assign volatile sig_atomic_t=1, saving old handlers. Create TCP listening socket, set O_NONBLOCK and FD_CLOEXEC explicitly using fcntl before listen; bind INADDR_ANY at htons(config.port); listen with config.backlog if positive else bounded default 128. Poll listener plus all clients. For each peer pollfd request POLLIN always and add POLLOUT only if its output FIFO is nonempty. Listener dispatch: call accept_clients only for listener POLLIN. For each peer: when POLLIN is present, call read_client, which drains recv until EAGAIN and handles complete frames in order. When POLLOUT is present and peer survives, call write_client, which drains queued bytes until EAGAIN/empty. Never call read_client solely for POLLOUT. On POLLHUP, first process POLLIN to drain complete bytes, then mark for removal after read_client processed all available complete frames; on EOF retain/process all complete buffered frames before discarding an incomplete tail and removing peer. POLLERR/POLLNVAL or fatal read/write marks only that peer for removal. Poll timeout is finite (e.g. 500ms) so signals are noticed; EINTR rechecks stop flag, other poll failure is fatal. During dispatch, set deletion flags rather than freeing a peer while poll-index traversal still references it; after event batch, unlink/free each marked client exactly once. On accept resource failure for an individual peer close that descriptor and continue accepting/servicing others; listener fatal error ends loop. If a recipient is idle but has queued output, POLLOUT remains requested independently of recipient POLLIN, so response/delivery progresses without additional input. Accepted descriptors do not rely on listener mode inheritance: accept_clients explicitly applies O_NONBLOCK and FD_CLOEXEC before publishing initialized peer into list. On SIGINT/SIGTERM stop accepting and polling, restore old handlers, close listener, and remove all clients, releasing fd/input/output/subscription resources.", "STATE_CHANGE": "During lifetime maintain one owned client list; each accepted connection starts disconnected MQTT state with empty input/output/subscriptions; successful CONNECT enters connected state; removal destroys per-connection Clean Session and every owned resource. Shutdown releases all remaining clients and listener.", "RESPONSE": "Returns 0 after signal-requested orderly termination. Returns -1 on fatal listener setup or poll failure after full cleanup. Per-peer protocol/transient failures remove only that client and do not fail broker_run. All installed signal handlers restored and all descriptors, buffers, queue nodes and filters freed before return.", "EVENT_TYPE": "poll-driven broker runtime lifecycle", "INVARIANTS_USED": ["R01: concurrent peer service on nonblocking TCP and independent readiness dispatch.", "R08: complete frames are dispatched before EOF cleanup.", "R10/R12: per-client teardown and process signal shutdown free all owned state.", "F05: preserve TCP ordered byte-stream semantics.", "F21: transient/protocol failure isolates offending connection."], "POSTCONDITION": "On every return listener fd is closed, client list is empty, old signal dispositions restored, and no connection-owned allocations remain."}
